# AquaGuard AI — Netlify SPA fallback.
#
# The client is a React Router single-page app: the server only ever serves one
# HTML file and the router owns every path after the first load. Netlify is a
# static host, so without this rule a visitor who opens or refreshes a deep link
# (e.g. /dashboard, /history, /admin) gets Netlify's own 404 — the file for that
# path does not exist on disk. That failure looks like "the deploy is broken"
# even though the bundle is fine.
#
# `200` (rewrite) rather than `301` (redirect) is deliberate: the browser URL
# stays as typed and index.html boots the router, which then renders the right
# page. A redirect would change the address and break deep links.
#
# Real files still win — Netlify serves an existing asset before applying this
# rule, so /assets/*.js and /favicon.ico are untouched.
/*  /index.html  200
